@h1 Quotes, code & rules h1@

Four block-level containers handle the set-apart pieces of a document: a
block quotation for epigraphs and pulled quotes, an aside for sidebars and
editorial notes, a preformatted block for verbatim text, and a horizontal rule
for a section divider. Each is a node, and each carries a sensible default look
from the theme.

@h2 Block quotations h2@

A block quotation sets a passage apart from the surrounding prose — an epigraph,
a pulled quote, a cited paragraph:

@@@pre
@blockquote
"Sleep is the golden chain that ties health and our bodies together."
— Thomas Dekker
blockquote@
pre@@@

The passage is wrapped in a paragraph inside the quote, and the theme adds a
left rule and a muted colour. Use it for a quotation you want to lift off the
page; for a short quoted phrase _inside_ a sentence, ordinary quotation marks in
prose are enough.

@h2 Asides h2@

An aside is a note beside the main thread — a sidebar, an editorial remark, a
tip. The nice thing is that you write it the same way whether it stands alone or
sits inside a sentence, and the renderer places it correctly either way:

@@@pre
The keeper, @aside a rare inline remark, aside@ kept his watch.

@aside
A standalone note, set off as its own block.
aside@
pre@@@

The first becomes an inline @code <aside> code@ woven into the paragraph; the
second becomes a block @code <aside> code@ of its own. In this documentation, the
tip boxes you see are asides with a class on them:

@@@pre
@aside(class note) 💡 A short tip the reader should not miss. aside@
pre@@@

The class hooks the theme's callout styling — see
@a |href /docs/write/styling/| Styling a@ for how a class reaches your CSS.

@h2 Preformatted text h2@

A preformatted block renders in a monospace font — handy for a code sample, a
fragment of data, or anything you want shown verbatim in a fixed-width face:

@@@pre
@pre function relight(lamp) { return lamp.on(); } pre@
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 An honest limit: the preformatted node renders monospaced,
but its body still passes through the normal paragraph-wrapping, so it is not a
strict "significant whitespace" block. For anything where exact indentation and
line breaks _must_ survive, build the file and check the output before relying on
it. aside@

@h2 Horizontal rules h2@

A horizontal rule is a self-closing divider — a visual break between sections
with no heading:

@@@pre
The first movement ends here.

@hr hr@

The second begins.
pre@@@

It is written @@@code @hr hr@ code@@@ and takes no body, producing a plain
@code <hr> code@.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _Using a greater-than sign for a quote._ A line that opens with a
greater-than sign is literal prose, not a quotation — Wit has no Markdown
blockquote leader. Use the block-quotation node. li@
@li _Expecting exact whitespace from the preformatted block._ It is monospaced,
not byte-exact; verify the build if layout matters (above). li@
@li _Forgetting a self-closing node has no body._ The rule node stands alone;
there is nothing to put between an open and a close. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

@ul
@li @a |href /docs/write/emphasis/| Emphasis a@ — marks inside a quotation. li@
